RunAsDaemon 0
SocksPort 0
BridgeRelay 1
Nickname WTBridgeLab
Log notice stdout
ServerTransportPlugin webtunnel exec /usr/local/bin/webtunnel-server
ExtORPort auto
DataDirectory /var/lib/tor

ORPort 9001
ServerTransportListenAddr webtunnel 0.0.0.0:15000
ServerTransportOptions webtunnel url=https://webtunnel.local/secret-path-wt-research-2026

AssumeReachable 1
PublishServerDescriptor 0
ContactInfo lab-student@webtunnel.local

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FIX B-3 (docs/05-final-review.md) -- the controlled target for the positive class.
#
# v2.1 pointed the WebTunnel generator at duckduckgo.com, check.torproject.org and Wikipedia
# while every negative class fetched the local mock server.  Content, RTT, response size and
# session length were therefore confounded with the class by DESTINATION, not by protocol:
# median duration 5.18 s vs 2.45 s, median bytes_up 55,566 vs 9-22k, and gate G4 failed at
# p ~ 1e-48 because a public website cannot be asked to return B_down bytes.
#
# A private address cannot be fetched through a public Tor exit (exit policies reject RFC 1918
# and the exit has no route into the lab network), so the fix is an onion service: the traffic
# never leaves the Tor network, and it terminates on the SAME nginx front end and the SAME
# FastAPI handlers the negative classes use.  The only remaining difference between the positive
# class and direct_web_browsing is the WebTunnel transport itself, which is the research question.
#
# The address is stable across restarts because /var/lib/tor is a named volume in
# docker-compose.yml.  Read it with:  docker compose exec tor-bridge cat /var/lib/tor/onion_decoy/hostname
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/onion_decoy
HiddenServicePort 80 172.20.0.20:8080
HiddenServiceVersion 3
